course: language
lesson: 6
title: Prepositions and conjunctions: little words that hold sentences together
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Prepositions are small words that show where, when or how things connect: on, under, in, behind, before, after, with; a pencil "on the desk" and a pencil "under the desk" are in very different places.
explain: Conjunctions are joining words: and adds, but shows a contrast, or gives a choice, so shows a result and because gives a reason.
explain: "I went home because I was tired" is one sentence; "Because I was tired." on its own leaves you waiting, because a because-part needs a main part to lean on.
explain: Prepositions are the least logical words in any language, so countries differ: the British say "at the weekend", Americans and Australians "on the weekend"; northern English says "I'm sat" for I'm sitting, and an Aussie meets you "at the servo this arvo".
explain: Some old "rules" are myths: ending a sentence with a preposition ("What are you looking at?") is natural English, and starting with And or But can be fine for effect.
fact: A preposition shows place, time or connection, like on, in, at, under, behind, before, after and with; it comes before a noun or pronoun, as in "under the desk".
fact: A conjunction joins words or parts of sentences: and adds, but contrasts, or gives a choice, so shows a result, because gives a reason.
fact: A main clause makes sense on its own; a subordinate clause, like "because I was tired", needs a main clause to make full sense.
fact: British English usually says "at the weekend"; American and Australian English usually say "on the weekend".
fact: In Britain, especially the North, "I'm sat" and "I'm stood" mean I am sitting and I am standing; formal writing uses sitting and standing.
fact: In Liverpool and some northern areas people sometimes say "going the shops" for "going to the shops"; writing uses the full form.
fact: In Australia "servo" means a petrol (service) station and "arvo" means afternoon; arvo is used in New Zealand too.
fact: The "rule" against ending a sentence with a preposition was copied from Latin by some writers in the 1600s and 1700s; English naturally says "What are you looking at?".
fact: A famous joke mocking that rule, "This is the sort of English up with which I will not put", is often credited to Winston Churchill, but nobody has proved he said it.
fact: Starting a sentence with And or But is not banned; good writers sometimes do it for effect.
fact: "Eat your peas or you won't get pudding" and "Eat your peas and you'll get pudding" make the same deal, but "or" sounds stricter and "and" sounds kinder.
fact: Bored of, bored with and bored by all mean the same; older writers often prefer with or by, and younger speakers mostly say of.
exercise: The teacher holds the pencil beneath the desk. Which preposition fits? | under | on | in | behind
exercise: What does a preposition show? | Place, time or connection | An action that someone does with their body | A feeling or idea | The name of a person
exercise: In "I like cake but I don't like jelly", what is "but"? | A conjunction showing a contrast | A preposition showing a place | A noun naming a food | A verb showing an action
exercise: Which conjunction gives a reason? | because | or | but | and
exercise: Harry writes "I went home. Because I was tired." How should he fix it? | I went home because I was tired. | I went home, Because I was tired. | I went. Home because I was tired. | Because. I went home I was tired.
exercise: Why can't "Because I was tired" stand alone? | It is a subordinate clause that needs a main clause | It has no verb in it, so it can't be a sentence | It has too many words | It starts with a capital letter
exercise: Ben says "on the weekend" and his English mate says "at the weekend". Who is right? | Both: British says "at", American and Australian say "on" | Only the English mate, because "on" is always wrong here | Only Ben | Neither, it should be "in the weekend"
exercise: Ivy's mum says "I'm stood at the bus stop." What does she mean? | I am standing at the bus stop | Someone stood me at the bus stop | I stood there yesterday | I am about to stand up
exercise: An Aussie kid says "Meet ya at the servo this arvo." What does he mean? | Meet you at the petrol station this afternoon | Meet you at the restaurant later on this evening | Meet you at the service tomorrow morning | Meet you at the server this April
exercise: Ayaan's grandad says "Never end a sentence with a preposition!" What is true? | It is a myth copied from Latin; English ends that way naturally | It is a law that every English school must follow | It is only allowed in questions about food | It was a rule in Old English
exercise: Is it proved that Churchill made the famous "up with which I will not put" joke? | No, it is often credited to him but not proved | Yes, it is recorded word for word in one of his speeches | Yes, he wrote it in a book of jokes | No, it was said by Shakespeare
exercise: Rosa says "Eat your peas or you won't get pudding" and "Eat your peas and you'll get pudding" feel the same. What is the difference? | "Or" sounds stricter, "and" sounds kinder | One gets you pudding, the other never does | "Or" means you get two puddings | There is no difference at all, even in feel
exercise: Dev hears "bored of", "bored with" and "bored by". Which is correct? | All three are used and mean the same | Only "bored of", the others are wrong | Only "bored with" | Only "bored by"
exercise: A girl from Liverpool says "I'm going the shops, are you coming or wha?" What should the written form be? | I'm going to the shops | I'm going at the shops | I'm going on the shops | I'm going in the shops
exercise: Can a sentence start with "But"? | Yes, sometimes, for effect | Never, it is against the law | Only in poems | Only if it ends with a question mark
exercise: In "After lunch we hid behind the shed with the dog", how many prepositions are there? | Three: after, behind, with | Two: behind and with | Four: after, we, behind, with | One: behind
exercise: Which conjunction makes this true? "It was raining, ___ we took umbrellas." | so | but | or | because
exercise: "We stayed in ___ it was raining." Which conjunction fits best? | because | but | or | so
test: Your mate goes "prepositions are well pointless, tiny words innit." What sentence shows they matter? | "The cat is in the box" and "The cat is on the box" mean different things | "The cat sat" needs no small words, so they can always go | Prepositions only matter in French | Small words never change meaning
test: A Yorkshire dad says "I'm sat in t'car, get a move on!" (In Yorkshire "t'" means the.) What's going on? | He is sitting in the car waiting | Someone has sat him down in the car | He is about to get out of the car | He sat in the car yesterday
test: An Aussie mate says "Heaps of us are going to the beach this arvo, ya keen?" ("Heaps" means lots, "keen" means up for it.) When are they going? | This afternoon | This morning | Tomorrow night | Next weekend
test: Your teacher writes "What did you step on?" and Grandad says it breaks a rule. What's the best reply? | Ending with a preposition is natural English; the rule is a myth | Grandad is right, it must be "On what did you step?" in every case | It is fine only in America | It is fine only if you whisper it
test: What would happen if we had no conjunctions? | We'd have to say everything in short separate sentences | Nothing, because nouns would simply join the sentences | Every sentence would become a question | We could no longer use verbs
test: A Kiwi says "I'll see you on the weekend, chur." ("Chur" means thanks, cheers, in New Zealand.) Your nan from Kent says he got it wrong. Is that fair? | No, "on the weekend" is normal outside Britain | Yes, "at the weekend" is the only proper English anywhere | Yes, it should be "in the weekend" | No, but only because he said chur
test: Harry writes "We went to the beach. Because it was sunny. And we swam." Which part can't stand alone as a sentence? | Because it was sunny | We went to the beach | And we swam | None of them can stand alone
test: Your mum, mad, says "Tidy your room or no telly." Your dad, chilled, says "Tidy your room and you can have telly." Is the deal different? | No, same deal; mum's sounds stricter | Yes, Dad's deal gives you more telly than Mum's | Yes, Mum's means no telly ever | No, and both sound equally kind
test: In a game, the hint says "The key is under the rug behind the sofa." Where do you look first? | Beneath the rug that is behind the sofa | On top of the sofa, right above where the rug lies | Behind the rug in front of the sofa | Inside the sofa cushions
test: A US cousin says "I'm bored of school, lowkey." ("Lowkey" means a bit, quietly.) Your gran says "bored WITH". Who is wrong? | Neither; both are used | Your cousin, "bored of" is wrong | Your gran, "bored with" is wrong | Both, it must be "bored by"
test: Little sister writes "I like apples and I like pears and I like plums and I like grapes." How could she join it better? | I like apples, pears, plums and grapes | I like apples because pears plums grapes | I like apples but pears or plums so grapes | I like and apples and pears and plums
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a preposition? | A word indicating relations of place, time or connection, preceding a noun or pronoun | A word denoting an action or state, preceding a noun or pronoun | A word qualifying a noun | A word replacing a noun to avoid repetition
exam: Identify the principal function of the conjunction "because". | The introduction of a reason | The expression of a contrast | The presentation of an alternative | The indication of a location
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the placement of a preposition at the end of an English sentence. | Such placement is natural in English; the contrary prescription derives from imitation of Latin | Such placement constitutes an error in every variety of English, without exception | Such placement is permitted exclusively in interrogative sentences | Such placement was prohibited by statute in the eighteenth century
exam: Given the utterance "I went home. Because I was tired.", ascertain the correction most consistent with the conventions of written English. | I went home because I was tired. | I went home. Because, I was tired. | Because I went home. I was tired. | I went. Home because I was tired.
